  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Edwards developed an early fascination with speed through hydroplane racing and later studied aerospace engineering. He worked on classified United States Air Force research before moving to Hawaii, where he practised naval architecture and built large catamarans for day-charter operators. Exposure to pioneering multihulls shaped his approach to light structures and offshore performance.\n\nHe conceived Rave as a record-seeking ocean racer and persisted after an earlier hull was destroyed by Hurricane Iniki. The resulting trimaran used unusual forward-set floats, a tall carbon wing mast and seamless filament-wound composite tubes. Edwards led a specialist team through design and construction, making the project a distinctive meeting of aerospace methods, experimental naval architecture and private boatbuilding.",
